---
import PageLayout from "../layouts/PageLayout.astro";

const siteUrl = "https://zentype.jonathanrreed.com";
const pageUrl = `${siteUrl}/whats-new/`;
---

<PageLayout
	title="What's new in Zen Typer"
	seo={{
		title: "What's New in Zen Typer",
		description: "Release notes for Zen Typer. 1.1 is a redesign around the passage itself, with real typefaces, custom text, a smoother caret, and a paper theme. 1.0 brought living backgrounds, keyboard sounds, and 322 sourced quotes.",
		canonical: pageUrl,
		keywords: ["Zen Typer changelog", "Zen Typer release notes", "typing app update"],
		breadcrumbItems: [
			{ name: "Mindful typing test", url: `${siteUrl}/` },
			{ name: "What's new", url: pageUrl },
		],
	}}
>
	<p slot="lede" class="prose-lede">
		Everything here still runs entirely in your browser: no account, no server, nothing uploaded.
	</p>

	<section class="prose-release" id="v1-1">
		<p class="prose-meta"><time datetime="2026-11">November 2026</time></p>
		<h2>Zen Typer 1.1</h2>
		<p>
			A redesign. The first version put the quote in a box in the middle of a toolbar. This one takes the box and
			the toolbar away, so what is left is the passage, set the way it would be in the book it came from.
		</p>

		<section>
			<h3>The page</h3>
			<p>
				The passage is now the page. It is set large in Newsreader, a serif made for reading, with the author, the
				book, and the translator underneath. The header, the stats, and the footer are small type at the edges, and
				they dim while you are typing and come back when you move the pointer.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section>
			<h3>Real typefaces</h3>
			<p>
				The old font menu listed ten fonts and shipped none of them, so most people saw whatever their computer
				substituted. The site now carries its own: Newsreader and Literata if you like a serif, Hanken Grotesk and
				Atkinson Hyperlegible if you do not, JetBrains Mono if you want fixed width. Only the one you pick is
				downloaded.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section>
			<h3>Typing feel</h3>
			<p>
				The caret glides from letter to letter instead of jumping, and each letter takes its ink as you type it. A
				wrong key marks the letter you missed and nothing moves, so the line you are reading never reflows under
				you. Enter brings the next quote when you finish one.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section>
			<h3>Your own text</h3>
			<p>
				Paste a paragraph, a speech, or the opening of a book and practice on that. Line breaks, curly quotes, and
				long dashes are converted to what a keyboard can type. It replaces a browser prompt that could do none of
				that.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section>
			<h3>Washi</h3>
			<p>
				A ninth theme and the first light one: ink on paper, two ranges of hills in diluted ink, and a red sun. For
				daylight, or for anyone who never wanted a dark screen.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section>
			<h3>Zen mode</h3>
			<p>
				The writing box is now a single line to write on. Words lift off it in columns close to where you are
				looking, and they no longer land on top of each other.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section>
			<h3>Smaller things</h3>
			<ul>
				<li>Settings are sorted into five short tabs, with plain names for everything in them.</li>
				<li>The home page lists every book the passages come from, and how many each contributes.</li>
				<li>The menus, sliders, and switches are the browser's own, restyled, so phones get their native pickers and the app downloads less.</li>
				<li>The share image matches the new look.</li>
				<li>The session markers and "restore from ghost" tools are gone. Zen mode already saves everything you write as a draft.</li>
			</ul>
		</section>
	</section>

	<section class="prose-release" id="v1-0">
		<p class="prose-meta"><time datetime="2026-09">September 2026</time></p>
		<h2>Zen Typer 1.0</h2>
		<p>The biggest update since the site went up.</p>

		<section>
			<h3>A background that is alive</h3>
			<p>
				Every theme is now drawn on the fly by a small program on your graphics card: Void's aurora ribbon and stars, Cosmic's nebula with a shooting star every so often, Aurora's curtains, Ocean's light through water, Glacier's sheen and snow, Forest's light shafts and fireflies, Ember's rising sparks, Sakura's petals. The scene leans toward your cursor and brightens a little while you type. It uses a fraction of the power the old animated layers did, freezes to a single frame when you ask for reduced motion, and falls back to a still gradient where the graphics card is not available.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section>
			<h3>Sound that feels like a keyboard</h3>
			<p>
				Six switch profiles, each with a separate press and release, a body that depends on where the key sits on the board, and a room around it. Thock, cream, holy panda, clicky, typewriter (with a bell on Enter), and a raindrop for the quiet days. Four background sounds that move instead of looping: rain with real drops, wind with gusts, fire with crackles, and a low drone. Off by default. Ctrl+M or the speaker button turns it on, and it stays how you left it.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section>
			<h3>322 quotes, every one with its source</h3>
			<p>
				Marcus Aurelius, Epictetus, Seneca, Lao Tzu, Zhuangzi, Confucius, the Dhammapada, Kabir, Thoreau, Emerson, Whitman, Montaigne, Pascal, Boethius, Socrates, Okakura's Book of Tea, Nitobe's Bushido, and a handful of old proverbs. All from public-domain editions, translator and year named. Filter the pool by length or by theme in Settings. The made-up lines and the internet misattributions are gone.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section>
			<h3>Progress</h3>
			<p>
				Press Ctrl+P, or open it from the pause menu or the end of a quote. Days practiced, your current run of days, words written, time spent, a sixteen-week calendar, and a line of your recent speeds. If you keep the stats bar hidden, the view goes quiet too: days and words, no speeds.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section>
			<h3>Take it with you</h3>
			<p>
				Settings now has a backup button. One file holds your settings, history, and every draft with its versions. Restore it in another browser and everything merges in. There is also a plain delete-everything button, because your data should be as easy to remove as it is to keep.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section>
			<h3>Phones</h3>
			<p>
				Quote mode works on a phone now. Tap the quote and the keyboard opens; typing through an on-screen keyboard is handled properly, including autocorrect and suggestions. The header fits on one row, the stats bar stays in view, and everything pinned to the bottom lifts above the keyboard.
			</p>
		</section>

		<section>
			<h3>Smaller things</h3>
			<ul>
				<li>The target-pace marker in Quote mode is visible, and Timed Flow responds to its setting without a reload.</li>
				<li>Zen sessions count toward your history when you close the tab, not only when you reset from the pause menu.</li>
				<li>The clock in Zen mode starts on your first keystroke, so the words-per-minute figure is honest.</li>
				<li>Finishing a quote at any accuracy keeps your streak. The old 95 percent gate was invisible and confusing.</li>
				<li>Reset Session in the pause menu works again. It had not rendered in a built page for a while; it now banks the session and starts you fresh.</li>
				<li>A one-line hint on the first visit, then never again.</li>
				<li>A share card that looks like the app, with the quote on it.</li>
			</ul>
		</section>

		<section>
			<h3>Under the hood</h3>
			<p>
				About 2,000 lines of CSS animation left. One settings store replaces the ad hoc event wiring between islands. The test suite was replaced with one that checks the flows people actually use, in a real browser, including the reload paths that used to break silently. Source is on <a href="https://github.com/JonathanRReed/zen-type" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub</a>.
			</p>
		</section>
	
	</section>
</PageLayout>
